\ Exact month length composition for the selected year.
\ If a year has L days and m months, choose an ordered sequence
\ (L1,...,Lm) with 4 <= Lj <= 123 and sum Lj = L.
\ The sequences are ranked lexicographically by their day lengths.
\ No actual month may be longer than 123 days or shorter than 4.
\
\ Subtract four from each Lj. The new lengths are in 0..119.
\ The number of sequences of R days and P months is the coefficient
\ of x^(R-4P) in (1+x+...+x^119)^P. Inclusion/exclusion gives:
\   sum(j=0..floor((R-4P)/120)) (-1)^j *
\   C(P,j) * C(R-4P-120j+P-1,P-1).
\ All terms are nonnegative integers. Accumulate the positive and
\ negative terms separately to avoid signed large-number arithmetic.
\ The final subtraction is exact and nonnegative.
\ Use the number of suffix completions to unrank the desired sequence;
\ do not enumerate the complete, potentially huge solution space.
\ CAL compilation and execution have not yet been verified.

A month length record is a thing with
  a number called month length days.

The chosen month lengths are some month length records.
The new month length record is a month length record.

A month length work has
  a number called extra days,
  a number called excluded blocks,
  a number called maximum excluded blocks,
  a number called remaining days,
  a number called remaining months,
  a number called candidate days,
  a number called maximum candidate days,
  a number called part selected,
  a number called valid selection,
  a wide quantity called positive terms,
  a wide quantity called negative terms,
  a wide quantity called count result,
  a wide quantity called suffix possibilities,
  a wide quantity called total possibilities,
  a wide quantity called current rank.

The month length work is a month length work.

\ The count result is exact, or zero if no composition is feasible.
To count month lengths given a number and another number:
  Put "0" into the month length work's count result.
  If the other number is 0,
    count the empty month suffix given the number; exit.
  If the other number is less than 0, exit.
  Put the number into the month length work's extra days.
  Put the other number into a minimum month days number.
  Multiply the minimum month days number by 4.
  Subtract the minimum month days number from the month length work's extra days.
  If the month length work's extra days is less than 0, exit.
  Put the other number into a maximum extra days number.
  Multiply the maximum extra days number by 119.
  If the month length work's extra days is greater than the maximum extra days number, exit.
  Put "0" into the month length work's positive terms.
  Put "0" into the month length work's negative terms.
  Put 0 into the month length work's excluded blocks.
  Loop.
    Put the month length work's excluded blocks into an excluded days number.
    Multiply the excluded days number by 120.
    If the excluded days number is greater than the month length work's extra days, break.
    If the month length work's excluded blocks is greater than the other number, break.
    Add the next month counting term given the other number.
    Add 1 to the month length work's excluded blocks.
  Repeat.
  Put the month length work's positive terms into the month length work's count result.
  Subtract the month length work's negative terms from the month length work's count result.

To count the empty month suffix given a number:
  If the number is 0, put "1" into the month length work's count result.

\ The current inclusion/exclusion term is
\ C(P,j) * C(S-120j+P-1,P-1).
To add the next month counting term given a number:
  Calculate a binomial coefficient given the number and the month length work's excluded blocks.
  Put the binomial result into an inclusion term wide quantity.
  Put the month length work's extra days into a adjusted excess number.
  Put the month length work's excluded blocks into an excluded days number.
  Multiply the excluded days number by 120.
  Subtract the excluded days number from the adjusted excess number.
  Add the number to the adjusted excess number.
  Subtract 1 from the adjusted excess number.
  Put the number into a remaining slots number.
  Subtract 1 from the remaining slots number.
  Calculate a binomial coefficient given the adjusted excess number and the remaining slots number.
  Multiply the binomial result by the inclusion term wide quantity.
  Put the month length work's excluded blocks into a parity number.
  Divide the parity number by 2 giving a quotient number and a remainder number.
  If the remainder number is 0,
    add the inclusion term wide quantity to the month length work's positive terms; exit.
  Add the inclusion term wide quantity to the month length work's negative terms.

\ Count suffixes with the candidate first month fixed.
To count the current month suffix:
  Put the month length work's remaining days into a suffix days number.
  Subtract the month length work's candidate days from the suffix days number.
  Put the month length work's remaining months into a suffix months number.
  Subtract 1 from the suffix months number.
  Count month lengths given the suffix days number and the suffix months number.
  Put the month length work's count result into the month length work's suffix possibilities.

To remember the selected month length:
  Allocate memory for the new month length record.
  Put the month length work's candidate days into the new month length record's month length days.
  Append the new month length record to the chosen month lengths.
  Subtract the month length work's candidate days from the month length work's remaining days.
  Subtract 1 from the month length work's remaining months.
  Put 1 into the month length work's part selected.

\ Choose one month in ascending length order and skip rejected blocks.
To consider the current month length:
  If the month length work's suffix possibilities is numerically smaller than the month length work's current rank,
    subtract the month length work's suffix possibilities from the month length work's current rank;
    add 1 to the month length work's candidate days; exit.
  Remember the selected month length.

To choose the next month length:
  Put 0 into the month length work's part selected.
  Put 4 into the month length work's candidate days.
  Put the month length work's remaining months into a other months number.
  Subtract 1 from the other months number.
  Multiply the other months number by 4.
  Put the month length work's remaining days into the month length work's maximum candidate days.
  Subtract the other months number from the month length work's maximum candidate days.
  If the month length work's maximum candidate days is greater than 123,
    put 123 into the month length work's maximum candidate days.
  Loop.
    If the month length work's candidate days is greater than the month length work's maximum candidate days, break.
    Count the current month suffix.
    If the month length work's suffix possibilities is "0",
      add 1 to the month length work's candidate days.
    If the month length work's suffix possibilities is not "0",
      consider the current month length.
    If the month length work's part selected is 1, break.
  Repeat.

To select month lengths from a wide quantity:
  Put 0 into the month length work's valid selection.
  Destroy the chosen month lengths.
  If the wide quantity is "0", exit.
  Put the wide quantity into the month length work's current rank.
  Put the chosen year's year length into the month length work's remaining days.
  Put the selected month count into the month length work's remaining months.
  Count month lengths given the month length work's remaining days and the month length work's remaining months.
  Put the month length work's count result into the month length work's total possibilities.
  If the month length work's total possibilities is "0", exit.
  If the month length work's total possibilities is numerically smaller than the month length work's current rank, exit.
  Loop.
    If the month length work's remaining months is 0, break.
    Choose the next month length.
    If the month length work's part selected is not 1, exit.
  Repeat.
  Put 1 into the month length work's valid selection.

\ Questions about the structure all use sauce for (c, first day).
\ Rebuild the shared sauce if a preceding gate walk overwrote the bowls.
\ Bowl 3 / seal 31 chooses an index among all bounded compositions.
To choose the canonical month lengths:
  Prepare the year structure sauce.
  Put the chosen year's year length into a whole year days number.
  Count month lengths given the whole year days number and the selected month count.
  Put the month length work's count result into the month length work's total possibilities.
  If the month length work's total possibilities is "0", exit.
  Put 3 into the seal inquiry's bowl identity.
  Put 31 into the seal inquiry's seal number.
  Begin a sealed inquiry.
  Choose an equal option given the month length work's total possibilities.
  Select month lengths from the seal inquiry's selected quantity.
